The problem statement is to segregate even and odd elements in a linked list. To solve this problem, we can use a two-pointer approach. We will maintain two pointers, one for even elements and one for odd elements. We will traverse the linked list and for each node, we will check if its value is even or odd. If the value is even, we will add it to the end of the even list. If the value is odd, we will add it to the end of the odd list. Finally, we will return the head of the even list.

Here is the Java code for the solution:

```java
// Definition for singly-linked list.
public class Node {
    int data;
    Node next;
    Node(int data) {
        this.data = data;
        this.next = null;
    }
}

public class Solution {
    public Node segregate(Node head) {
        if (head == null) return head;
        Node evenStart = null;
        Node evenEnd = null;
        Node oddStart = null;
        Node oddEnd = null;
        while (head != null) {
            if (head.data % 2 == 0) {
                if (evenStart == null) {
                    evenStart = head;
                    evenEnd = head;
                } else {
                    evenEnd.next = head;
                    evenEnd = evenEnd.next;
                }
            } else {
                if (oddStart == null) {
                    oddStart = head;
                    oddEnd = head;
                } else {
                    oddEnd.next = head;
                    oddEnd = oddEnd.next;
                }
            }
            Node next = head.next;
            head.next = null;
            head = next;
        }
        if (oddStart == null) return evenStart;
        evenEnd.next = oddStart;
        return evenStart;
    }

    public static void printList(Node node) {
        while (node != null) {
            System.out.print(node.data);
            if (node.next != null) System.out.print(" -> ");
            node = node.next;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Node head = new Node(0);
        head.next = new Node(1);
        head.next.next = new Node(4);
        head.next.next.next = new Node(6);
        head.next.next.next.next = new Node(9);
        head.next.next.next.next.next = new Node(10);
        head.next.next.next.next.next.next = new Node(11);

        printList(head);
        System.out.println("\n");
        Node head2 = segregate(head);
        printList(head2);
    }
}
```

This code first initializes two lists, `evenStart` and `oddStart`, which will store the nodes of the even and odd lists, respectively. Then, it traverses the linked list and for each node, it checks if its value is even or odd. If the value is even, it adds the node to the end of the even list. If the value is odd, it adds the node to the end of the odd list. Finally, it returns the head of the even list.
